    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and the full suite is green (264 tests, 0 failures).\n\n## Summary\n\nI added a structured address form to the owner create/update flow while keeping the flat `address` input backward-compatible.\n\n**API contract** (`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`)\n- Added `addressLine1` and `addressLine2` (both optional strings) to the `OwnerFields` schema; these also appear on the read-time `Owner` response via `allOf`.\n- Rewrote the `address` description as the composed/normalized value (still accepted flat on input, always returned).\n- Removed `address` from the `required` list so a structured-only request (no flat `address`) passes generated `@Valid` schema validation. `city` stays required.\n\n**Persistence**\n- `model/Owner.java`: added `addressLine1`/`addressLine2` columns with getters/setters.\n- Added `address_line1`/`address_line2` columns to all four schema files (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres).\n\n**Function pipeline** (`rest/function/owner/`)\n- `ValidateOwner`: an owner is now valid when it supplies an address in **either** form \u2014 a non-blank normalized `addressLine1` **or** a non-blank normalized flat `address` \u2014 plus `city`. Minimal flat owners still pass.\n- `NormalizeOwnerAddress`: when `addressLine1` is present it is preferred \u2014 both structured lines are normalized (trim/collapse/upper-case/expand abbreviations) and the flat `address` is composed as `addressLine1` + (`\" \" + addressLine2` when present); otherwise only the flat `address` is normalized. Because the composed value is written back to `owner.address`, downstream reads (household hash keyed on postcode, postcode validation, locality \u2014 all keyed on the shared `city`/`postcode`) use the structured-derived value, falling back to the flat address.\n- `ApplyOwner` (PUT path): now carries `addressLine1`/`addressLine2` onto the entity.\n\nThe new `AcceptanceTest` cases both pass: structured input `\"10 king st\"` yields `addressLine1` = `\"10 KING STREET\"` and a composed `address` = `\"10 KING STREET\"`, and the flat form is still accepted.\n\nOne scope note: the legacy JDBC repository's explicit INSERT/UPDATE SQL doesn't list the two new columns (its `SimpleJdbcInsert` picks them up from table metadata but the hand-written `UPDATE` does not), so a JDBC-profile PUT won't persist the structured lines. The active OfficeFloor endpoints and all tests run on the spring-data-jpa profile, where they persist correctly \u2014 but flag it in case the JDBC arm ever needs parity.",
